\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Turk lira hits 5mth-high, bucks cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2002\par 16:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The Turkish lira closed at a five-month high on Monday on hopes for \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's EU membership bid, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell lira and dampen lira volatility. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices also rose, driving yields to their lowest levels this year, while stocks rose modestly. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted to weaken the lira since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in the wake of a devastating financial crisis in February 2001. The bank has moved in the past to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The lira ended the day at 1,523,000 to the dollar, off an earlier high at around 1,510,000 but beating Friday's 1,531,000 close, its best since June 11. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had moved to arrest a persistent rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after bankers told Reuters it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for lira. \par \par \uc2  "A direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market took place today aimed at preventing an observed fluctuation in the exchange rate," the bank said in a written statement. \par \par \uc2  While lira strength helps Turkey's fight against inflation, it is a handicap for exporters who are expected to drive the country's return to strong growth after the damage of financial crises in 2000 and 2001. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Friday it would restart auctions to buy dollars if political conditions continued to fuel lira strength. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Sureyya Serdengecti warned earlier last week that investors taking positions on the lira could get their fingers burned. \par \par \uc2  The lira hit a low of some 1,735,000 to the dollar in early November before rallying on the landslide election win for the Justice and Development Party (AKP), Turkey's first one-party government in 15 years. \par \par \uc2  EU OPTIMISM \par \par \uc2  The main factors driving lira strength are high real interest rates on a massive domestic debt load and optimism for Turkey's European Union membership bid. \par \par \uc2  Bond yields fell around three percentage points to their lowest levels this year on Monday as investors bet on a measure of Turkish success for its drive to win a date to start membership talks when the bloc meets at a December 12 summit in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  Average yields on December 3, 2003, debt fell to 47.16 percent from a previous close of 50.4 percent. The July 2 papers, also busy, were down to 49.34 percent. \par \par \uc2  Investors said expectations for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut, possibly before the Copenhagen summit, were also helping to drive bond yields down. \par \par \uc2  "Right now investors are focusing on the Copenhagen story and they are coming round to our view Turkey's going to get a result at Copenhagen," said Tolga Ediz, director in global economics at Lehman Brothers in London. \par \par \uc2  Real interest rates on lira assets are still very high, given that inflation looks set to fall well below a 35 percent target for consumer inflation set alongside the IMF. November inflation data are due for release on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The treasury auctioned 1,195.3 trillion lira (around $788) 169-day debt on Monday at a maximum yield of 49 percent, in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  Turkish stocks recovered from earlier falls to close up 0.48 percent at 13,364.17 points with otherwise positive sentiment dampened by worries for the completion of IMF-backed bank reform, compromised by a November court decision to suspend the bank watchdog's seizure of Pamukbank. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021202dyc200ojy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
