\par\par \b \uc2  Dervis pledges low profile role in Turkish "troika". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mark Bentley \par \uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2002\par 18:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, July 13 (Reuters) - Economy Minister Kemal Dervis, torch-bearer of Turkey's $16 billion IMF pact, signalled on Saturday he would keep a low profile in a new centre-left bloc that is staking its claim to power in the crisis-hit country. \par \par \uc2  The United States and European Union, which candidate Turkey aspires to join, have expressed their concern as an unfolding government crisis threatens stability in NATO's sole Muslim state, currently leading peacekeeping efforts in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Seven ministers, including ex-foreign minister Ismail Cem, and more than 37 deputies have fled ailing premier Bulent Ecevit and his Democratic Left Party (DSP) this week, reducing the largest party in parliament to third largest. Further departures are expected. \par \par \uc2  "As long as I remain in government it is not possible to take an active role in the new movement...One has to think about Turkey's economic interests," Dervis told the daily Hurriyet. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's western allies see the presence of Dervis, an ex-World Banker, in the crumbling coalition government as the closest thing to a guarantee the IMF's biggest debtor will implement a reform plan to escape the worst economic recession since 1945. \par \par \uc2  The 77-year-old veteran premier has brushed off talk of early elections his nationalist allies have called for November, insisting his apparently divided three-party government could reach agreement on a dispute over EU reforms. \par \par \uc2  "We must continue with this government until the end," he said in a live television interview on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Ismail Cem, who quit Ecevit's party and resigned as foreign minister this week, said in a statesman-like speech on Friday he had formed a new social democratic "troika" with former deputy premier Husamettin Ozkan and Dervis, brought in after a February 2001 financial crisis ripped through Turkey's economy. \par \par \uc2  Cem pledged on Saturday to support the non-party technocrat as he worked to implement Turkey's IMF programme. \par \par \uc2  "Dervis is very important for this government and he has a very important function to perform," the state-run Anatolian news agency quoted Cem as telling reporters in Ankara. \par \par \uc2  "Appraisals we conduct with Mr. Dervis will be done within a framework of respect (for his position)." \par \par \uc2  The ex-foreign minister said he would hold a meeting in the Turkish capital with defected MPs focusing on efforts to set up a new political party ahead of the next general election. \par \par \uc2  WOLFOWITZ VISIT \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Defense Secretary Paul Wolfowitz will visit Turkey on Sunday before flying on to Afghanistan. A U.S. defence official, who asked not to be identified, told reporters the visit had no connection with Turkey's political turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "They (the Turks) have a functioning government and we have an important relationship," the official said. Wolfowitz would likely broach the issue of Iraq, Turkey's eastern neighbour, he added, amid speculation about a possible U.S. military invasion to depose President Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  Ankara is a staunch supporter of Washington's declared war on terrorism and allows U.S. and British warplanes to patrol a no-fly zone in northern Iraq from an airbase in southern Turkey. \par \par \uc2  Cem said Ecevit's government was no longer able to tackle the country's problems and democratic reforms Ankara must pass to begin membership talks with Brussels. \par \par \uc2  "There is an impasse on the EU issue. We will certainly provide the greatest support to this government or another government," he said. \par \par \uc2  Reacting to news Dervis had joined Cem's camp, coalition Nationalist Action Party (MHP) leader Devlet Bahceli declared on Friday the economy minister's "marginal use had been wiped out" and demanded Ecevit and \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president accept his resignation. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Thursday to prop up the free-floating lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when Dervis temporarily quit. \par \par \uc2  Dervis later returned to office on the appeal of President Ahmet Necdet Sezer, meeting with an IMF delegation visiting Ankara for the latest review of Turkey's pact. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the emerging political picture in Ankara may compromise Turkey's economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  "We are worried the IMF programme may take a back seat as Turkey's politics are realigned," Lehman Brothers said in a research note. "The battle... will be carried out at the ballot box. And the elections will effectively be a referendum on EU accession." \par \par \uc2  NEW HAWKISH FOREIGN MINISTER \par \par \uc2  Ecevit expressed his eagerness this week to press ahead with reforms to open the European Union's gates to Turkey but then appointed Sina Sukru Gurel, a man with a hawkish reputation on Cyprus and the EU, as his new foreign minister. \par \par \uc2  Greek Foreign Minister George Papandreou said in a letter to Gurel late on Friday he hoped recent improvements in relations between their nations could continue. \par \par \uc2  Gurel, also installed as deputy premier in Ecevit's revamped cabinet, says his country's foreign policy will not change. \par \par \uc2  Greece and Turkey have been at odds for decades over territorial issues in the Aegean and divided island of Cyprus. \par \par \uc2  "No changes in any of the policies are being considered... Of course we will continue with our foreign policy, keeping with the known principles of this government's programme and the coalition agreement," Gurel told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Credit ratings agencies Moody's and Standard and Poor's downgraded their outlooks on Turkey to negative this week because of the mounting political turmoil. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020713dy7d00hr3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
