sit Turkey on Sunday before flying on to Afghanistan. A U.S. defence official, who asked not to be identified, told reporters the visit had no connection with Turkey's political turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "They (the Turks) have a functioning government and we have an important relationship," the official said. Wolfowitz would likely broach the issue of Iraq, Turkey's eastern neighbour, he added, amid speculation about a possible U.S. military invasion to depose President Saddam Hussein. \par \par \uc2  Ismail Cem, who quit Ecevit's party and resigned as foreign minister this week, said in a statesman-like speech on Friday he had formed a new social democratic "troika" with former deputy premier Husamettin Ozkan and Dervis, brought in after a February 2001 financial crisis ripped through Turkey's economy. \par \par \uc2  Cem said Ecevit's government was no longer able to tackle the country's problems and democratic reforms Ankara must pass to begin membership talks with Brussels. \par \par \uc2  Reacting to news Dervis had joined Cem's camp, coalition Nationalist Action Party (MHP) leader Devlet Bahceli declared on Friday the non-party technocrat's "marginal use had been wiped out" and demanded Ecevit and \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president accept his resignation. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Thursday to prop up the free-floating lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when Dervis temporarily quit. \par \par \uc2  Dervis later returned to office on the appeal of President Ahmet Necdet Sezer, meeting with an IMF delegation visiting Ankara for the latest review of Turkey's pact. \par \par \uc2  NEW HAWKISH FOREIGN MINISTER \par \par \uc2  Ecevit expressed eagerness this week to press ahead with reforms to open the European Union's gates to Turkey but then appointed Sina Sukru Gurel, a man with a hawkish reputation on Cyprus and the EU, as his new foreign minister. \par \par \uc2  Greek Foreign Minister George Papandreou said in a letter to Gurel late on Friday he hoped recent improvements in relations between their nations could continue. \par \par \uc2  Gurel, also installed as deputy premier in Ecevit's revamped cabinet, says his country's foreign policy will not change. \par \par \uc2  Greece and Turkey have been at odds for decades over territorial issues in the Aegean and divided island of Cyprus. Cem and Papandreou have been credited for a spectacular thawing of relations in the past three years. \par \par \uc2  "No changes in any of the policies are being considered... Of course we will continue with our foreign policy, keeping with the known principles of this government's programme and the coalition agreement," Gurel told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Ecevit met with Gurel on Saturday at his official residence, but no statement was made after the meeting. \par \par \uc2  Credit ratings agencies Moody's and Standard and Poor's downgraded their outlooks on Turkey to negative this week because of the mounting political turmoil. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook reflects concerns that political instability could persist and jeopardise the IMF-supported economic programme," S&P said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020713dy7d00f4l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
