\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Dervis pledges low profile role in Turkish "troika". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mark Bentley \par \uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2002\par 16:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, July 13 (Reuters) - Economy Minister Kemal Dervis, torch-bearer of Turkey's $16 billion IMF pact, signalled on Saturday he would keep a low profile in a new centre-left bloc that is staking its claim to power in the crisis-hit country. \par \par \uc2  The United States and European Union, which candidate Turkey aspires to join, have expressed their concern as an unfolding government crisis threatens stability in NATO's sole Muslim state, currently leading peacekeeping efforts in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Seven ministers, including ex-foreign minister Ismail Cem, and more than 37 deputies have fled ailing premier Bulent Ecevit and his Democratic Left Party (DSP) this week, reducing the largest party in parliament to third largest. Further departures are expected. \par \par \uc2  "As long as I remain in government it is not possible to take an active role in the new movement...One has to think about Turkey's economic interests," Dervis told the daily Hurriyet. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's western allies see the presence of Dervis, an ex-World Banker, in the crumbling coalition government as the closest thing to a guarantee the IMF's biggest debtor will implement a reform plan to escape the worst economic recession since 1945. \par \par \uc2  The 77-year-old veteran premier has brushed off talk of early elections his nationalist allies have called for November, insisting his apparently divided three-party government could reach agreement on a dispute over EU reforms. \par \par \uc2  "We must continue with this government until the end," he said in a live television interview on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Ismail Cem, who quit Ecevit's party and resigned as foreign minister this week, 