\par\par \b \uc2  Dervis pledges no active role in Turkish "troika". \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2002\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, July 13 (Reuters) - Economy Minister Kemal Dervis, the torch-bearer of Turkey's $16 billion IMF pact, signalled on Saturday he would keep a low profile in a new centre-left bloc that has staked its claim to power in the crisis-hit country. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's western allies see Dervis's presence in Turkey's crumbling coalition government as the closest thing to a guarantee the IMF's biggest debtor will implement a reform plan to pull Turkey out of its worst economic recession since 1945. \par \par \uc2  "As long as I remain in government it is not possible to take an active role in the new movement... One has to think about Turkey's economic interests," Dervis told the daily Hurriyet. \par \par \uc2  The United States and European Union, which candidate Turkey aspires to join, have expressed concern a political crisis sparked by an exodus from premier Bulent Ecevit's government is threatening stability in NATO's sole Muslim state, currently leading peacekeeping efforts in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Ismail Cem, who quit Ecevit's party and resigned as foreign minister this week, said on Friday he had formed a new social democratic "troika" with Dervis, an ex-World Banker, and former deputy premier Husamettin Ozkan. \par \par \uc2  The 77-year-old Ecevit has brushed off talk of early elections, insisting his apparently divided coalition could reach agreement on a dispute over EU reforms that has led his nationalist coalition allies to call for November polls. \par \par \uc2  Nationalist Action Party (MHP) leader Devlet Bahceli on Friday demanded Ecevit and Turkey's president accept Dervis's resignation, saying news the non-party technocrat had joined Cem's camp meant his "marginal use had been wiped out". \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the free-floating lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when Dervis temporarily resigned. \par \par \uc2  Dervis returned to office on Friday on the appeal of President Ahmet Necdet Sezer, meeting with an IMF delegation visiting Turkey for a latest review of the economic programme. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020713dy7d00ccm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
