\par\par \b \uc2  Dervis sees Turkey meeting export goal, demand up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ercan Ersoy \par \uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2002\par 15:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, April 5 (Reuters) - Economy Minister Kemal Dervis said on Friday crisis-hit Turkey could meet an IMF-backed export target for 2002 despite discouraging recent data and predicted a modest recovery in domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Turkey is looking to exports to achieve economic growth of three percent in 2002 after its economy contracted 9.4 percent in 2001. But exports fell 13 percent year-on-year in February according to preliminary customs data. "The moderate recovery will be driven by a reasonable increase in domestic demand, nothing spectacular, in the order of three to four percent perhaps, and a reasonably good export performance," Dervis told an Istanbul conference. \par \par \uc2  "From the programme we foresee export growth of between six and seven percent is achievable." \par \par \uc2  The preliminary data showed imports were down 35 percent in February, and a Reuters poll of industrial production for that month indicates no immediate recovery in domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  The survey sees a 4.2 percent decline in industrial production in February after falls of 3.1 percent in January. \par \par \uc2  Dervis has said Turkey must resist the urge to stimulate economic growth at the expense of cutting consumer inflation to a targeted 35 percent by year-end from 65.1 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  On Friday he ruled out any government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to encourage a cheaper lira, something \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's firms have sought as a means to increase export growth. \par \par \uc2  Dervis said a gradual rise in imports would help the lira towards a dollar rate that could help stimulate exports. \par \par \uc2  "It will be necessary in the medium term to have a competitive real exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Turkish lira recently hit highs of some 1,290,000 to the dollar after slumping to an all-time low of 1,650,000 lira to the dollar last October. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at best bids of 1,306,000 against the greenback on the local interbank market on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The lira has lost close to 50 percent of its value since it was floated amid last year's financial crisis. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sureyya Serdengecti told the same Istanbul conference the bank was not yet happy with expectations for inflation but stressed disinflationary goals would not be sacrificed in the name of growth and exports. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020405dy4500qvd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
