\par\par \b \uc2  Turk shares end down on debt worries. \b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 17:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Oct 18 (Reuters) - Turkish shares slid more than one percent on Thursday after a government minister said Turkey needed to urgently restructure its debt, but held above key support levels while the market awaits fresh foreign loan news, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Sales mostly hit leading mobile phone operator Turkcell , which fell 10.29 percent to 6,100 lira after recent sharp gains chasing New York stocks. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund on Wednesday said it was too soon to talk about increased lending for Turkey but it was in broad agreement with Ankara on budget targets for 2002. Turkey is hoping to secure up to $13 billion in foreign cash to plug a financing gap in its 2002 budget. \par \par \uc2  The main ISE National-100 index ended 1.59 percent lower at 8,818.09 points after fluctuating between 8,724 and levels approaching 9,000 points. It had gained nearly 6.3 percent during the previous three trading days. \par \par \uc2  "The index has been testing the 8,900-9,000 levels for the last two days. The main resistance is at 9,100. In order to pierce these levels, we need to see goverment decisiveness and positive remarks about additional loans," said Kadir Ozkaya at Acar Securities in Istanbul. \par \par \uc2  After Industry Minister Ahmet Kenan Tanrikulu called for urgent restructuring of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's domestic debt mountain, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the lira, sending bonds lower. \par \par \uc2  Tanrikulu later said he had been misunderstood, but brokers said the initial panic had been enough to affect sentiment in equities. \par \par \uc2  "This statement led to a rise in bond yields and falls in the index too," said Ebru Sengul of Datek Securities. \par \par \uc2  Consumer electronics and television exporter Vestel was the most active share, rising 2.15 percent to 2,375 lira. The stock is still enjoying the effects of Tuesday's decision by the European Commission to terminate anti-dumping proceedings on imports of televisions from Turkey. \par \par \uc2  Mostly state-owned flat steel maker Eregli Demir Celik fell five percent to 14,250 lira after posting losses of 173.63 trillion lira ($106 million) in the first nine months of 2001 compared with an 81.7 trillion lira net profit in the same period of 2000. \par \par \uc2  Private commercial bank Garanti Bankasi closed flat at 1,425 lira. Its merger with fellow group bank Osmanli was announced this week. Italy's IntesaBci said on Monday negotiations with Garanti continued but it was not yet considering taking a stake. \par \par \uc2  Daily turnover rose slightly to 483.9 trillion lira (around $297 million) from Wednesday's 461.5 trillion lira. Of the 289 shares traded, decliners outnumbered gainers by 140 to 98, while 51 ended unchanged. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dxai07390\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
