\par\par \b \uc2  TurK CB steps in after debt comments shake markets. \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 14:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Oct 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the lira on Thursday after a minister sent crucial bond yields spiking higher and drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower with comments he said were misunderstood as calling for domestic debt restructuring. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action lasted until the close and until bids came up to around 1,630,000 lira (to the dollar)," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. \par \par \uc2  The lira had fallen as low as 1,650,000 on the independent interbank market after Industry Minister Ahmet Kenan Tanrikulu urged in a public speech that: "Work on restructuring the domestic debt must be started rapidly". \par \par \uc2  The minister later said he had been misunderstood and such a thing was out of the question. Economy Minister Kemal Dervis also released a statement saying there had been no change in Turkey's debt management policy. \par \par \uc2  "After that the market calmed down a bit," said another \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The lira had closed on Wednesday at 1,637,000 to the dollar on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -brokered spot market . It closed on that market at 1,630,000 after deals of around 1,642,000 in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The minister's remarks also sent yields on benchmark bonds spiking more than a percentage point to 88 percent in some trades in a thin market. \par \par \uc2  Average yields on benchmark March 6, 2002, papers were 87.20 percent on Thursday after closing Wednesday just below 86 percent. \par \par \uc2  Any increase in yields is bad news for Turkey's treasury, which is trying to use billions of dollars in IMF support to drive down the cost at which it borrows. \par \par \uc2  The main share index in Istanbul shrugged off the comments, which came during the break between sessions. \par \par \uc2  Shares were trading just under one percent lower with eyes there still fixed on the international lending Turkey says it will need next year to help it roll over a domestic debt stock that reached 101,814 trillion lira (around $65 billion) by the end of August. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dxai05mso\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
