\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Turkish  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  props lira, curbs share losses. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Steve Bryant \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2001\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The first direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the lira's float succeeded in propping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up on Friday, and helped equities claw back some early-session losses. \par \par \uc2  The bank sold dollars after the lira hit a record low of 1,652,000 to the dollar on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -brokered spot market , helping to meet Turkish investors' seemingly insatiable demand for safe-haven hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first such action by the bank since the flotation in late February, pushed the lira to close at 1,580,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is putting in quotations on the market to buy lira for dollars," one banker told Reuters. Other traders also detected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, which came immediately before the spot market closed. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling at around 1,600,000 to the dollar," said another banker. \par \par \uc2  Shares, which had been down almost three percent, recovered slightly to close 2.64 percent lower. The main ISE National-100 index ended the day at 7,598.91 points. \par \par \uc2  "The morning rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices brought some selling, even if it was reluctant. After the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  some of these came back," said Kerim Sarc of ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been sliding for days, setting record after record as investors grew anxious over Economy Minister Kemal Dervis's talks with the IMF in Washington and over the likely impact for NATO member Turkey of any U.S. strikes in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Turkey will need billions of dollars in funding from either the IMF or private market sources in 2002. \par \par \uc2  CLUTCHING AT STRAWS \par \par \uc2  "I think they're clutching at straws here. What Turkey needs is a fresh injection of funds and it doesn't seem like they're anywhere near getting that yet," said a London-based analyst. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pledged to act to prevent "excessive" fluctuation in the value of the lira, which has lost more than half its value against the dollar since the February float. \par \par \uc2  But its room for manoeuvre is constrained by criteria of Turkey's $15.7 billion international rescue lending package, which limits how much \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves can fall over time. \par \par \uc2  The bank said in early September the limit for falls in net international reserves for the September and October period was $3.25 billion, comprising an IMF-agreed $2.5 billion limit and $650 million carried over from unused allowances August. \par \par \uc2  The bank said on Thursday its reserves had risen $1 billion to $18.925 billion in the week ending September 28, swollen by treasury deposits after a dollar and euro-denominated debt offering, indicating it may have more cash at its disposal for any support in defence of the lira. \par \par \uc2  Analysts in London said they needed to know the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before commenting on its importance. \par \par \uc2  "It's difficult to assess the motivation behind the move until we get some idea of the amount of dollars they sold. However, it could be justified as a temporary measure to rein in a market that looked to be getting out of hand," said another London analyst. \par \par \uc2  One local banker estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and state banks had sold around $150 million to prop up the lira on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Others questioned the wisdom of feeding a desire for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has seen shares and other lira-denominated instruments such as treasury bills avoided by many investors. \par \par \uc2  "It's not very good for credibility when they've said to the market we're only going to do it with dollar auctions," said Sheetal Radia at Standard and Poors MMS in London. \par \par \uc2  "They've now altered the market dynamics. They're selling what everyone wants to buy," Radia said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had previously restricted action to daily $20 million auctions, largely to process IMF cash and revenue from \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated bonds it sold to the market. \par \par \uc2  Average yields on the busiest paper maturing on March 6, 2002 rose more than one percentage point to 94.18 percent. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Mark Bentley, James Thornhill). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011005dxa500ezi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
