\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Turkish CB reassures markets on debt, lira. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Steve Bryant \par \uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2001\par 11:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assured markets on Friday the country could handle its hefty debt stock and said recent falls in the value of the lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The written statement, which followed an appearance on television by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Sureyya Serdengecti, appeared to ease markets, with the lira firming slightly and shares rising nearly two percent in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  "The current debt stock is one that can comfortably be rolled over," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The lira hit a record low of 1,515,000 to the dollar on Wednesday amid concerns for Turkey's ability to handle its debt load under a $15.7 billion IMF-backed crisis recovery pact. \par \par \uc2  Financial crises in November and February drove many banks to the wall and the country has issued billions in new domestic debt to bail out ailing state and private banks. \par \par \uc2  But interest rates have stayed well above levels foreseen under the IMF plan, forcing the treasury to raise its prediction of average 2001 borrowing rates to 91 percent from 81 percent and sparking market fears the debt could become unsustainable. \par \par \uc2  The statement stressed Turkey had the financial and monetary discipline needed to push the pact through. \par \par \uc2  "The basic condition of a stability programme, financial and monetary discipline, has been completely established," the statement said, adding first-half indicators pointed to Turkey reporting a primary budget surplus, which excludes payments on debt, of around the target of 11,568 trillion lira or some 6.3 percent of gross national product (GNP). \par \par \uc2  'BUBBLE' WILL BURST \par \par \uc2  The statement said the recent falls in the lira did not reflect those fiscal realities. \par \par \uc2  "A \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate that is not justified by economic fundamentals, and a trend to move even further above that level, is only a bubble. Bubbles always burst," it said, adding the switch to a floating rate system to cap market turmoil in February had created natural upheaval. \par \par \uc2  The lira has lost around 50 percent of its value since. \par \par \uc2  "It is only natural that changing the rules of the game creates tension among the players. It is certain that as the rules become accustomed, this tension will disappear," it said. \par \par \uc2  "We fully believe once the realities of the exchange rate movements are understood, the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will begin to behave like a normal market," the bank said. \par \par \uc2  It said it had spent $7 billion, partly to stabilise the lira, from the February float to July 31. Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been limited. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's IMF plan limits the amount the bank can spend each month to stabilise the lira. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010817dx8h00i24\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
