\par\par \b \uc2  Turkish CB head says rates too high, lira too low. \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2001\par 08:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sureyya Serdengecti said on Friday interest rates of over 90 percent were too high and would come down to 60 percent levels as confidence in Turkey's $15.7 billion IMF pact grows. \par \par \uc2  "Interest rates need to come to the 60 percent level - as doubts are eased, so will rates fall," Serdengecti said, speaking live on private news channel NTV before markets opened. Turkey needs lower interest rates to help it deal with a domestic debt load swollen after months of banking crisis. \par \par \uc2  Serdengecti also said recent falls in the value of the Turkish lira against the dollar were excessive but gave no details of how or when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The parameters of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's IMF deal limit the amount the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can spend each month to defend the lira \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The lira was floated amid financial crisis in late February and has lost around 50 percent of its value since. \par \par \uc2  It hit a record low of 1,515,000 to the dollar on Wednesday but has since recovered, particularly following a meeting on Thursday between Serdengecti and the heads of major banks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010817dx8h00c79\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
