\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Turkish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  faces dilemma as lira slides. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Asli Kandemir \par \uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, Aug 16 (Reuters) - A joke in Istanbul has bankers discussing what they would do if they learned the world was to end tomorrow. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese banker would make a last visit to the golf course, the French banker would visit his mistress, but the Turkish banker has a different plan: \par \par \uc2  "I'd buy dollars," he says. \par \par \uc2  It is a popular option in Turkey where doubts over debt sustainability and a 50 percent depreciation in the Turkish lira since it was floated in February have made the U.S. unit the safe haven of choice. \par \par \uc2  The lira hit all-time lows on Wednesday with dealers saying only determined \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intevention can stop further falls. \par \par \uc2  But the $15.7 billion IMF crisis rescue pact imposes limits on Turkey's ability to prop up the flagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through limits on how much foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves may fall each month. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should sell dollars in an effective manner. If not, I cannot guess how far the dollar can go," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a private sector local bank. \par \par \uc2  Economy Minister Kemal Dervis has said the slide is an overshoot and will "definitely reverse." Lira depreciation quickly feeds through into Turkey's stubbornly high rate of inflation since the country imports most of its fuel. \par \par \uc2  The lira hit a record low of 1,515,000 to the dollar on Wednesday, bringing the week's depreciation to 9.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  NO WIN SITUATION \par \par \uc2  Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned a policy of unannounced dollar sales in June as hefty purchases by \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banks rendered that policy of limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ineffective. \par \par \uc2  The bank then announced plans of pre-announced dollar sales for July and August to both help curb demand for the greenback and process IMF loans destined for public institutions. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers now criticise that revised system too, saying those sales also encourage buying. \par \par \uc2  "This auction system is being used as a speculative tool as banks push up prices before the auction," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Backing the demand from banks is a widespread public faith in the dollar as a hedge against inflation - running at over 60 percent annually in July - and against lira volatility. \par \par \uc2  The irony is that such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  substitution reinforces inflation and "increases the volatility of the floating exchange rate," according to a recent MSDW research note. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say Turkey's agreement with the IMF to abandon hefty \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the hallmark of exchange rate policy under a previous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, led the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to follow a less active and more transparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, making its dollar sales less effective. \par \par \uc2  According to the pledges given to the IMF, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can sell $2.5 billion in July and August including leftover allowances from previous months. \par \par \uc2  The bank says it sold $700 million in July and has already announced scheduled auctions to sell a total of $600 million in August, leaving it with leeway to sell more. \par \par \uc2  MORE THAN A BILLION IN HAND \par \par \uc2  Analysts say unused dollars earmarked for lira support in previous months will permit the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend well in excess of $1 billion in a possible lira rescue mission. \par \par \uc2  Tolga Atac of Bayindir Securities said small \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little chance of arresting the lira's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it comes down 30-40,000 (lira) but then new buyers step in," Atac said, adding that only large scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make a difference. \par \par \uc2  During the crisis in November the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $5 billion in a week with little impact on slowing demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  In a further effort to prop up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and increase confidence in lira-based assets, Turkey took measures in late July to encourage long term lira investments through tax breaks on long-term deposits and bond purchases. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say those measures came too late to stop dollarisation after the switch to a floating rate regime. \par \par \uc2  "You've got an environment of distrust. Nobody wants to own anything Turkish, any Turkish assets," Atac said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say dollarisation is also being fuelled by exporters reluctant to change foreign earnings to lira and, despite a recent $8 billion debt swap, banks are also purchasing the dollar to close their remaining open \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions. \par \par \uc2  Difficulty in rolling over syndicated dollar loans leads banks to borrow from the domestic market to repay that debt. \par \par \uc2  While large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the lira may carry both prohibitive cost and dubious result, some analysts say the Turkish government should give clearer signals about where it stands on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue. \par \par \uc2  "I think (having) no monetary policy would be better than this," said Selim Cevikel of CLSA in Istanbul, referring to the policy of limited dollar sales the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now follows. \par \par \uc2  "They're afraid the lira will get weaker but then they're not willing to defend it...It's the worst sign. Which \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does this kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" Cevikel said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00etq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
