\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Turk politics place hugh strain on IMF-rescue plan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Asli Kandemir \par \uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2001\par 13:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, July 12 (Reuters) - Turkey and the IMF look set to solve a row that has delayed the payment of an urgent loan, but domestic political rifts may still keep interest rates sky-high, putting a heavy strain the country's IMF-backed rescue plan. \par \par \uc2  A 10-day deadlock with the IMF - ostensibly over the composition of the board of landline monopoly Turk Telekom, but really a test of Turkey's commitment to reform - has eroded much of what market confidence remained in the country's fractious three-party coalition, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "The government's obstinacy with the IMF over the Telekom issue hurt market confidence a lot," said an economist with a foreign bank based in Turkey. "Everyone reckons a similar crisis could emerge any time, in a week, in a month." \par \par \uc2  The IMF and World Bank are expected to release more than $3 billion in loans on Thursday when they meet to review steps Turkey has taken to meet fund demands on bank reform and the grooming of Telekom for privatisation. \par \par \uc2  The dispute over the make up of the Telekom board highlighted hostility in the government's nationalist wing to Economy Minister Kemal Dervis, who brokered the rescue deal with the IMF and World Bank. Markets see the Nationalist Action Party (MHP) increasingly as an opposition within the government. \par \par \uc2  CRIPPLING INTEREST RATES \par \par \uc2  Turkish government debt yields - what it costs the country to borrow - are now punishingly high at around 100 percent, reflecting the market's lack of appetite. Investors have seen the value of lira-denominated bonds tumble as the lira has lost almost half its value since the February crisis struck. \par \par \uc2  Such yields, if sustained over coming months, could raise the country's debt burden to levels making the rescue programme unsustainable. \par \par \uc2  The domestic debt stock more than doubled to 84,500 trillion lira by end of May from 36,400 trillion lira at the end of last year and total interest payments will now account for upwards of 85 percent of government revenues in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Yet the crisis recovery plan, backed by $15.7 billion in IMF and World Bank loans, is predicated on a steady fall in rates. \par \par \uc2  "The current interest rate levels reflect investors' risk premiums. It's not because of high inflation expectations but lack of confidence in the programme's future," said Yarkin Cebeci, chief economist at Ottoman Bank. \par \par \uc2  Turkish wholesale inflation was 52.4 percent in June, while the most traded bond yields swing around 90-100 percent. \par \par \uc2  BLAME PUT ON LIRA FLOAT \par \par \uc2  A report by Nomura Equity Research was even gloomier. \par \par \uc2  "Turkey's economy is now clearly falling into severe financial stress. Higher bond yields are now a given, driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in a vicious circle. \par \par \uc2  "Inflows of fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  receipts from the tourism industry (this summer) are masking what is a very ugly dynamic. Once these tail off in September, unfortunately just in time for the opening of parliament, yields are likely to explode and the lira will head south." \par \par \uc2  Fears of an Argentinian debt default have also added to the economic pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The IMF appears reluctant to consider any further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up Argentina, fund sources arguing political damage is of the country's own making. The warning might apply also to Turkey, which is seen to be unlikely to receive a third international bail-out in under 12 months after packages in November and February. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts argue the floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate system, introduced when Turkey was forced to abandon a crawling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg regime in February, is the real cause of the current high interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Investors cannot guess where the exchange rate can go, so they ask for high risk premiums for their lira investments, pushing interest rates to levels higher than those under a system where exchange rates are predictable," wrote Ercan Kumcu, an economist and columnist at the daily Hurriyet. \par \par \uc2  Overnight interest rates, the only instrument the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can use to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are anchored at 63 percent. The bank cannot change the rate, since a cut would prompt dollar demand, and a rise would put a greater burden still on the treasury. \par \par \uc2  "It's true the floating rate puts pressure on interest rates," said Cebeci of Ottoman Bank. "But, if we were still in the crawling peg system, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves would disappear in a week under such political fears." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost about $6 billion of its international reserves since the February crisis. They now stand at $16.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  FLIGHT OF CONFIDENCE \par \par \uc2  Some other analysts argue interest rates should have fallen even under the floating exchange rate system with rising exports, falling inflation and a rosier current account balance. But that premium has been squandered by the politicians. \par \par \uc2  "With confidence so badly torn at home and abroad, we could not even enjoy those benefits the floating rate system could have brought," the foreign bank economist said. \par \par \uc2  The lira briefly traded at a new record low of 1,345,000 against the dollar on Thursday on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -brokered spot market - a fall of almost a half since February's float. \par \par \uc2  Turkish banks and investors still seek solace in dollars, as a safe haven in crisis periods. Banks have recently closed their open foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions after an $8 billion debt swap, and seem reluctant to turn to lira instruments again. \par \par \uc2  The high dolarisation in the system, makes restoring confidence in the lira much harder, analysts said. According to the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, 83 percent of its liabilities are now in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are at a turning point," said Funda Gungor, chief economist at Demir Bank. "If the government meets all commitments to the IMF, the programme may have a chance to survive, though hanging by a thread." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx7c00pc3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
