\par\par \b \uc2  Turkish stocks expected cautious after party ban. \b0\par\par\uc2 359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2001\par 08:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, June 25 (Reuters) - Turkish stocks are likely to react cautiously on Monday to a Constitutional Court ban on the main opposition Virtue Party as attention turns to a crisis-racked economy, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The Court expelled two members of the Islamist party from parliament, relieving investor worries of possible by-elections and doubts over financial reforms crucial for $15.7 billion in lending from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). "It is clear there is no longer a danger of early elections," said Mehmet Ascioglu of Demir Yatirim. "But we do not expect the exchange to rise above 11,250-11,300. There's an auction tomorrow and economic developments are the most important criteria for us now." \par \par \uc2  The ISE National-100 index rose 1.25 percent to close at 11,119.75 percent on Friday before the court made its decision later that evening. \par \par \uc2  The Turkish parliament has passed a swathe of reforms since signing a deal with the IMF in May. The fund is due to meet later on Monday to discuss Turkey's performance in adhering to a stringent economic programme. Brokers say worries over the weakness of the Turkish lira, which lost more than 40 percent of its value after a February crisis, will dampen any optimism in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "The result of the Virtue closure case has brought relief. \par \par \uc2  "There may be a slight rise today but increases will be dampened by the lira-\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dimlemma, demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to banks' syndicated loans, lack of cash for investment, and a general lack of confidence in the lira which is causing people to stay in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ozgecan Firildak of Kent Portfolio Management. \par \par \uc2  The index ended down 6.08 percent last week on worries over the closure case and the lira, which weakened to around 1,277,000 against the dollar by Friday, a depreciation of some 46 percent since it was floated in February. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can only be limited as the government aims to use a weakened lira to increase export earnings. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6p00uq3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
