\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Foreign money needed for Turkish rescue. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Servet Yildirim \par \uc2 1,002 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, March 15 (Reuters) - Turkey must secure billions of dollars in foreign loans to tackle the chief cause of its recent crisis - weakness in the state-dominated banking system. \par \par \uc2  "We no longer have the luxury of doing this \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  without foreign support," Ercan Kumcu, a leading economist, wrote in a column for Hurriyet newspaper. \par \par \uc2  A failure to acquire this foreign financing would force the government to rely more heavily on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resources, printing money and pumping up inflation to perilous levels. \par \par \uc2  According to Morgan Stanley Dean Witter, Turkey urgently needs foreign financing for bank restructuring to avoid a debt restructuring. It did not give any further estimate about the type of debt restructuring. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say Turkey, with a good past record of foreign debt servicing, is not likely to consider foreign debt restructuring but it may look for ways of rolling over its high domestic debt redemption in coming months. One idea is to convert maturing debt into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -indexed bonds. \par \par \uc2  "The Treasury may find relief in giving foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  papers in return for short-term Turkish lira bonds in bank portfolios," Deniz Gokce of Bosphoprus University said. \par \par \uc2  STATE BANKS TARGET OF REFORM \par \par \uc2  Economy Minister Kemal Dervis unveiled on Wednesday government plans to reform state banks which account for a third of the whole banking system in terms of asset size, a total of $144 billion at the end of September. \par \par \uc2  He did not detail the magnitude, means and sources of financing required but indicated banking would be the target of the injections. The World Bank and IMF have already made clear there can be no more loans with a convincing plan to sanitise the banking system. \par \par \uc2  "According to our estimates, the cost of an all-out and all inclusive clean up of banks would be prohibitively high - including the state banks, banks under administration and potential insolvent banks," said Ercan Uysal of SG EMEA Research. \par \par \uc2  "TRUSTWORTHY AND RESPECTABLE PROGRAMME" \par \par \uc2  There is still no clear sign from the IMF and World Bank of loans but Prime Minister Bulent Ecevit appears optimistic. \par \par \uc2  "I hope our preparation of a trustworthy and respectable programme and its rapid and determined implementation will put foreign resources into action," Ecevit told his deputies. \par \par \uc2  Turkey has so far secured an $11.5 billion IMF loan to carry out its programme devastated by the recent crisis. \par \par \uc2  Philip Poole, head of emerging markets at ING Barings, said the IMF itself was unlikely to contribute significant additional funds given the already sizeable commitment on Turkey's quota. \par \par \uc2  "However, the fact that the direct securitisation of the hole in the state banks has been set below the generally accepted estimate of the size of the problem probably means that additional resources will be forthcoming from the World Bank specifically for this purpose," Poole said. \par \par \uc2  "DUTY LOSSES" BURDEN BANKING SYSTEM \par \par \uc2  Dervis said the government would set up a joint board of independent banking professionals for three state banks - Ziraat, Halk and Emlakbank. The plan proposes an eventual merger of Ziraat and Emlak. \par \par \uc2  The three banks, used by the government as an instrument to subsidise some sectors, has total "duty losses" of around 14,000 trillion lira ($14 billion). These losses place a heavy burden on the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The government plans to eliminate the duty loss stock by issuing government bonds to the banks, short of cash. \par \par \uc2  Most of the state banks must borrow heavily on overnight markets. \par \par \uc2  "This is an urgent problem which threatens the banking system," a text prepared by Dervis's office said. The text, headlined "The measures needed to be taken within the framework of economic programme", was distributed to journalists at Dervis' press conference on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The three banks should be provided with a total resource of 13,000 trillion ($13 billion) for the solution of the problem." \par \par \uc2  It said another $3 billion should be injected into the banks to boost their capital adequacy. \par \par \uc2  Such a radical and costly plan has probably been endorsed by the World Bank and IMF which have been urging Turkey for almost a decade to overhaul its troubled banking system. \par \par \uc2  CSFB estimates the cost of a banking bailout at around "$20-25 billion which is too high for Turkey to finance alone without printing money, which might pave the way for hyper-inflation. \par \par \uc2  "Although it is not only the state banks that will need recapitalisation, the state banks are likely to account for the bulk of the aggregate bailout need," CSFB said in a note. \par \par \uc2  "If the total for the state banks is $16 billion, then the total for the banking system as a whole is probably in the order of $20-25 billion." \par \par \uc2  CRISES RESOLUTION TO TEST BUSH \par \par \uc2  The Turkish crisis sets an early test for the new U.S. administration of President George W. Bush. The Bush camp has advocated a policy of less direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial crises, setting itself apart from President Bill Clinton. \par \par \uc2  This might bode ill for Turkey, which received $7.5 billion in new loans from the IMF after a crisis in December. Certainly the United States, while offering general support, has said nothing to commit it to any loans. \par \par \uc2  Professor Alan Makovsky of the Washington-based Institute for Near East Policy believes Turkey's position as a NATO ally at the rim of the Middle East gives it particular significance. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the 'ideological purity' of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to allow a crucial ally like \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  to collapse," he said. "That would have serious foreign policy consequences." \par \par \uc2  Uysal of SG anticipates a "partial rehabilitation and restructuring initiative spread over 2-3 years, which might require the liquidation of insolvent banks, the merger of small-to-medium sized commercial banks." \par \par \uc2  "Such an initiative would require a budget of $25-30 billion which can be financed through monetisation and, domestic and external debt financing. We expect a combination of these financing options with respective weights of 30, 40 and 30 percent," Uysal said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3f03xdh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
