\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  has offered cash at lower rates... \b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2001\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has offered cash at lower rates but the markets are still waiting for a long-term economic Plan B. \par \par \uc2  By Joseph Logan \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Feb 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisively on the money market, offering cash at low interest rates to soothe markets and ease pressure on its newly floated lira. \par \par \uc2  The first lira dollar deals on the interbank market were recorded at 970,000, a depreciation of some 29 percent since the float of the lira last week, only slightly weaker than late Monday, but up from losses of 36 percent in the first days of free trade. At around 1030 GMT the lira stood at 985,000. \par \par \uc2  Turkey abandoned its controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the heart of an anti-inflation programme backed by over $11 billion in IMF loans, last week after a political row sparked panic, sending interest rates to astronomical levels and crushing the peg. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to provide cheap credit and abundant liquidity, as it did on the previous day, offering banks three-day dollar and euro lending at 40 percent and overnight lira liquidity at 150 percent in the interbank money market. \par \par \uc2  The first deals on that market were at 100 percent, plunging from around 5,000 percent last week as cash-strapped banks scrambled for liquidity at any price. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the strategy of the bank, whose chief Gazi Ercel resigned at the weekend after the demise of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy he helped design, was paying off in the short term but that the pressure was on to design an economic policy from the ruins of the plan built around the lira peg. \par \par \uc2  Treasury Undersecretary Selcuk Demiralp, another architect of the gutted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, has also submitted his resignation. Deputies are filling both posts until Turkey's coalition government agrees on successors. \par \par \uc2  "The markets are still short of lira and dollars, people are just trying to survive rather than trying to make money," said Burak Akbulut, a strategist with Istanbul's Bayindir Securities. \par \par \uc2  "The markets are still waiting for a package, a plan B from the government. It's been almost a week that we haven't heard anything clear from the government in terms of what the monetary policy is going to be in this new environment. \par \par \uc2  "Will Turkey follow the old disinflation programme, will that programme survive, or to what extent are we going to obey the rules of the old programme?" he said. \par \par \uc2  The main share index , which has surged since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float as investors pumped money into the shares of tourism companies and others seen benefiting from a weaker lira, was down about one percent, as brokers said profit taking set in. \par \par \uc2  "Tomorrow is the last opportunity to get into cash (before a religious holiday next week)," said Alper Ozge of General Fianans. \par \par \uc2  BANK SECTOR SOLUTION NEEDED \par \par \uc2  Akbulut said all Turkish markets welcomed the steps taken till now to cap the crisis, but that a cogent policy that dealt with the country's bloated, creaking banking sector had to emerge quickly or those gains would evaporate. \par \par \uc2  "The markets are not going to buy the expectations, they're going to behave according to solid steps rather than expectations," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the high rates generated by the financial crisis have boosted pressure on Turkish banks, and that the devaluation may have pushed some with heavy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  obligations to the wall. \par \par \uc2  The head of the country's banking watchdog, which has seized 11 troubled banks, at the weekend said all banks had managed to close their positions. Bankers said much of the sector, including two state giants, had been unable to do so last week. \par \par \uc2  Akbulut said speculation in the media about plans to merge some of the state banks and let some of the shakier Turkish banks go under might be a bitter pill that Turkish policymakers would have to swallow to win the trust of markets. \par \par \uc2  "That's a very radical solution but that's what Turkey should do...a radical and healthy solution is the troubled banks should be wiped out of the system whatever the cost," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx2r05bzr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
