\par\par \b \uc2  Turkish Lira Float Not A Threat To Asia - Salomon \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2001\par 09:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Turkey's decision to float its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the aftermath of a political crisis in unlikely to have wide ramifications for Asia, as there are few trade links between Turkey and this region, Salomon Smith Barney said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Routes for contagion to Asia from Turkey's decision are not wide," Salomon Smith Barney said in a report. \par \par \uc2  Salomon noted that total trade between Turkey and Asia was only around $4 billion in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's exports to Asia are less than 0.5% of the total goods the country sells overseas, with many of these items usually headed for India and South Korea, Salomon said. \par \par \uc2  Financial problems in one emerging market often spill over into neighboring countries and other emerging markets, as investors become more risk-averse. \par \par \uc2  That was the situation in Asia in 1997 when the devaluation of the Thai baht in July quickly led to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation in Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, and South Korea. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Turkey was forced to abandon exchange rate controls and allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely. The Turkish lira lost 36% of its value as early as Friday, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovering some of its losses early this week following an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Turkey\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decided to float the lira after political infighting between\par the Prime Minister Bulent Ecevit and President Ahmet Necdet Sezer\par triggered a massive loss of confidence in the economy, which translated\par to huge outflows of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by nervous investors.\par - - 27/02/01 08-55G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, Salomon Smith Barney said concerns over Turkey's political\par and economic stability is not likely to trigger new fears in Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Certainly there is fragility in some of the region's banking systems,\par but nothing that should be new or surprising to investors," the company\par said, citing Indonesia, Thailand and China.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par More so, Salomon Smith Barney said characteristics of the Asian\par financial crisis like huge current account deficits, appreciated\par exchange rates, and off-track reform programs are absent in the current\par scenario.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Although the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of China, Malaysia and Hong Kong are somewhat\par pegged, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  environment in these three countries are\par different from that of Turkey, the company said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For one, Salomon Smith Barney said Hong Kong's banking system and\par fiscal accounts are "solid."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While Malaysia's banking system is not as sound as Hong Kong's, there\par has been no major deterioration over the past years, Salomon Smith\par Barney said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China's structural problems in its fiscal and banking systems,\par meanwhile, aren't immediate worries for investors, the company said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Rita Raagas De Ramos, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2832-2331;\par Rita.DeRamos@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010709dx2r007qw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picsc