\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey stepped in to calm crisis-racked markets and revive the beleaguered lira ... \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ralph Boulton \par \uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2001\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, Feb 26 (Reuters) - Turkey stepped in to calm crisis-racked markets and revive the beleaguered lira on Monday but top-level talks between political and military leaders produced no sign its premier and president had settled a power struggle. \par \par \uc2  "It was a nice, beneficial, fruitful meeting," Prime Minister Bulent Ecevit told reporters after a meeting of the powerful National Security Council (MGK), scene last Monday of a row that shook markets and changed the face of Turkey in a week. \par \par \uc2  He gave no details before going to talks with government partners and an MGK statement made no reference to the row. \par \par \uc2  Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , whose governor resigned at the weekend over the collapse of the country's IMF-backed financial reforms, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to force interest rates down by lending money to banks well below last week's sky-high levels. \par \par \uc2  Average deals on the repo market - used to inject money into the system to ensure smooth transactions between banks - were 122 percent compared to 1,300 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move in a crisis that has reverberated through international emerging markets immediately affected market mood. The squeeze of last week when some banks had trouble squaring accounts eased. \par \par \uc2  FEARS LINGER FOR THE FUTURE \par \par \uc2  Stocks, which lost a third of their value last week after Ecevit accused Sezer of prompting a crisis by belittling his anti-corrution campaign, soared by up to eight percent. The lira, which slumped 36 percent after it was floated on Thursday, clawed its way back to 28 percent below pre-crisis levels. \par \par \uc2  The influx of funds will be welcomed by an 80-strong banking system, Achilles heel of the Turkish economy. A liquidity squeeze stirred a banking crisis in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  last November defused only by IMF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a $7.5 billion loan package. \par \par \uc2  Milliyet newspaper said in an unsourced report on Monday that the IMF, whose Turkey desk head is in Ankara to discuss a new counter-inflation programme, may offer more emergency loans. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that Monday's action would ease immediate fears on the markets, but dangers remained. Banks could use "cheap money" to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Again, Turkey could be faced with the rush to dollars that shook the system last week. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcements will go some way towards cooling the markets' fever," said Yarkin Cebeci, chief economist at Ottoman Bank. \par \par \uc2  "This is what we need for the short term, but...we need a revised economic programme for the medium term and we also need the political will to defend it," he added. \par \par \uc2  Political uncertainty lingered, however, after the meeting of the secretive MGK, a body used by powerful generals to impart their views to politicians, especially at times of crisis. \par \par \uc2  For Ecevit and President Ahmet Necdet Sezer, it was the first encounter since Ecevit abandoned the last MGK meeting on Monday accusing Sezer of belittling his efforts to fight graft. He declared a crisis of state that was all too promptly picked up by markets sensitive to recurring political instability. \par \par \uc2  Those present at the abortive meeting said passions ran so high that a book, the constitution, was thrown around - an image not edifying to generals, markets or the general populace. \par \par \uc2  Ecevit has fended off demands that he sack ministers, clearly fearing any reshuffle could upset the delicate balance of his three-party coalition. But \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gazi Ercel resigned on Friday and official sources said Treasury head Selcuk Demiralp had also decided to go. \par \par \uc2  ECEVIT REMAINS IN SADDLE \par \par \uc2  While Ecevit has been widely faulted for his conduct last week, when key interest rates soared over 5,000 percent, the belief still lingers on markets that his departure could create political chaos and paralyse government. \par \par \uc2  The generals have made it clear they do not want to become directly or publicly involved in the dispute. However Ecevit and Sezer would in effect have been giving account to the General Staff as well as to markets at the MGK meeting. \par \par \uc2  The MGK statement, however, dwelt on Turkey's reform programme to prepare for European Union membership talks - something that seems now more distant following the crisis. \par \par \uc2  IMF Turkey desk head Carlo Cottarelli is in Ankara for urgent talks on measures to renew Turkey's disinflation programme, which had been designed to cut inflation to single figures by the end of next year from up to 100 percent in the 1990s. \par \par \uc2  Humour at least has not died. \par \par \uc2  Milliyet daily carried a front-page cartoon showing Ecevit being searched as he entered the MGK meeting room and hundreds of copies of the constitution spilling onto the floor. \par \par \uc2  "Sorry, sir," says the security officer. "No copies of the constitution in the meeting room." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx2q058v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
