\par\par \b \uc2  Turkish markets numbed by earthquake. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mete Akcok \par \uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1999\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Turkish bonds yields rose and the lira weakened on Wednesday on concerns about the economic cost of the huge earthquake that crippled the country's most industrialised region. \par \par \uc2  "There was an overall lack of appetite. But there was also dollar demand fuelled by foreign investors' sales in the bond market," said Gokhan Yilmaz, trader at Denizbank. \par \par \uc2  Yields climbed to 97.41 percent, up 3.3 percentage points from their levels before Tuesday's quake. The lira closed at 439,800 against the dollar compared with Monday's close of 438,100. Istanbul's stock exchange has suspended trading until next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Bond sales come mainly from foreigners...due to worries about the effects of the quake on the economy," said Tugrul Ozbakan, Capital Markets deputy manager at Turk Ekonomi Bankasi. \par \par \uc2  Officials say it is too early to give a figure for the total financial cost of the quake which devastated a large area in western Turkey. The earthquake has so far cost some 3,500 lives, including 460 in Turkey's financial capital of Istanbul. \par \par \uc2  It has severely interrupted commercial life in some quake-hit towns like industrial city of Izmit and Adapazari, severing power and telephone links. A tyre factory of Italian Pirelli collapsed near Izmit. \par \par \uc2  Izmit, the epicentre of the quake measuring 7.4 on the Richter scale, is home to many ventures involving foreign companies in the automobile, petrochemical and machinery sectors including companies like Honda , Toyota , Hyundai , Pirelli, Goodyear , Mannesmann  and Lafarge . \par \par \uc2  The quake will also set back the privatisation programme the International Monetary Fund wanted the country to start as one condition for funds. The Tupras oil refinery in Izmit, the country's biggest and top of the privatisation list, has been ablaze since the quake. \par \par \uc2  "It's normal that there will be reactionary sales by foreigners in the bond market, but this must be temporary as this is nothing related to macroeconomics," said Yilmaz. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in support of the lira on Wednesday afternoon, as it often does to control the rate of depreciation of the lira. Bankers estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold around $400 million to buy lira. \par \par \uc2  A treasury bond auction was also hurt by the gloom. Average and maximum yields for the three-year floating-rate notes rose to 127.62 and 130.91 percent from 119.54 and 123.25 percent respectively. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8i02f3m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
