\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey bank law just step toward reform-JP Morgan. \b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 1999\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, July 1 (Reuters) - Turkey's banking law, passed by parliament last month, is a step in the right direction but more measures are needed to complete reform of the sector, an economist at JP Morgan said. \par \par \uc2  "It is too early to declare that banking reform has been achieved," economist Emin Ozturk said in a recent research note. \par \par \uc2  "If anything, the latest legislative changes may be considered as just the first step in a necessary reform process that in the next step will require careful implementation of the new law with minimum political interference." \par \par \uc2  The law creates a regulatory and supervisory body for the 75-member banking system to monitor policy implementation and rule on the establishment and closure of banks. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are concerned that the body could be shaped by political interests. \par \par \uc2  "Good implementation will depend on the skill of the body and, particularly in the Turkish context, on the absence of political \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its decisions," Ozturk said. \par \par \uc2  He said \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also reduce the public sector's share in banking and rehabilitate the undercapitalised state banks which try to finance most of the off-budget deficits. \par \par \uc2  Four state-owned commercial banks make up more than one-third of the system's assets which rose to $116.7 billion at end-1998. \par \par \uc2  "Moreover, all these steps would need to be complemented by a fiscal reform, since high public sector borrowing requirements almost inevitably create major balance sheet risks for the private banks," Ozturk said. \par \par \uc2  The banking sector carries a large portfolio of government securities and a considerable level of short \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, estimated at around $9 billion. \par \par \uc2  Ozturk pointed at the risks of this policy. \par \par \uc2  "One is the risk of a step devaluation or a faster-than-expected depreciation of the lira exchange rate. The other risk arises from the mismatch of maturities on asset and liability sides." \par \par \uc2  On the asset side, the average maturity of the Treasury's borrowing is over one year. On the liability side, maturities of deposits are mainly at around three months while most repurchase agreements (repos) with customers carry maturities from overnight to one month. \par \par \uc2  He said "the deplorable shape" of Turkey's public finances had created strong incentives for banks to carry high levels of such risks. \par \par \uc2  "Reduction of those incentives through a cut in the borrowing requirement of the public sector is a sine qua non for effectively addressing the balance sheet risks in the banking sector." \par \par \uc2  The high cost of lira funding increased by the public sector borrowing need, reserve requirements and taxes on deposits, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of keeping lira depreciation in line with inflation make short \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions attractive for banks. ((Servet Yildirim, Istanbul Newsroom, 90-212-275 0875, fax 90-212-267 2137, istanbul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv71055x5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
