\par\par \b \uc2  Key facts about Turkey. \b0\par\par\uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 1999\par 09:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ANKARA, April 18 (Reuters) - These are the key facts about Turkey, where parliamentary elections are taking place on Sunday: \par \par \uc2  POPULATION: 63 million. \par \par \uc2  AREA: Turkey's 780,576 square km (301,382 square miles) covers Asia Minor and the southeastern toe of Europe. Bordered by Greece and Bulgaria to the northwest, Iran, Georgia and Armenia to the east and Iraq and Syria to the southeast. \par \par \uc2  CAPITAL: Ankara. \par \par \uc2  LANGUAGE: Mainly Turkish. About six million Kurdish speakers and several hundred thousand Arabic speakers. \par \par \uc2  RELIGION: Ninety-nine percent Moslem, mostly Sunni. Alevi Moslem population of roughly 10 million. Small Jewish, Armenian and Greek Orthodox communities. \par \par \uc2  GOVERNMENT: A constitutional republic with a 550-seat parliament as the sole legislative authority. General elections held at least every five years. The president, currently Suleyman Demirel, is elected by parliament for seven-year terms. \par \par \uc2  POLITICS: Incumbent Prime Minister Bulent Ecevit came to power in January, 1999, as a caretaker to guide the country to April 18 elections, held more than a year before they would normally be due. The last general elections were held in 1995. \par \par \uc2  An Islamist party was narrowly the winner of those elections and an Islamist-led coalition emerged after the collapse of a deal between conservative rivals Tansu Ciller and Mesut Yilmaz. But pressure from the powerful secularist military, a major political force, edged the coalition between Ciller and the Islamists from power in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Since then weak secularist coalitions under Yilmaz and then leftist Ecevit have ruled over Turkey's estrangement from the European Union. The same period has also seen an increasingly stern foreign policy force climbdowns from Syria and Cyprus. \par \par \uc2  However, with the Islamists effectively isolated in parliament by military distrust, coalitions between feuding leaders became increasingly hard to build and consensus was reached on the need to hold early elections. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMY: Turkey witnessed a sharp decline in gross national product growth to 3.9 percent last year after rapid growth of around eight percent in recent years. The global financial crises which prompted the slowdown also contributed to downward pressure on chronic high inflation. Anti-inflationary government programmes helped bring annual wholesale price inflation down to 48.2 percent in March from 86.0 percent a year earlier. While keeping the lid on inflation and boosting economic activity, the new government will be expected to rein in gaping public deficits. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains a policy of steady lira depreciation in line with inflation, maintained by near-daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The slowdown in growth has also limited growth in exports, which rose to $26.88 billion in 1998 from $26.26 billion the previous year. The trade deficit shrank to $19.04 billion from $22.30 billion last year. \par \par \uc2  The budget deficit fell to 7.0 percent of gross national product in 1998, from 7.6 percent a year earlier. The current account surplus in 1998 stood at $2.69 billion, compared with a deficit of $2.64 billion a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The political uncertainty of recent months has put privatisations on hold after some progress in sell-offs last year. The government raised some $1.0 billion from privatisations in 1998. \par \par \uc2  MODERN HISTORY: The Turkish Republic was declared in 1923 from the ashes of the Ottoman Empire. The key figure in establishing the new nation was former army officer Mustafa Kemal, later named Ataturk (Father of Turks), whose memory is still widely revered. \par \par \uc2  Kemal led the nationalist movement to victory over occupying powers which dismembered the empire after its defeat in World War One. Until his death in 1938, Kemal, first president and virtual dictator, oversaw Turkey's transformation into a secular, one-party state aimed at Western-style development. \par \par \uc2  Turkey was neutral in World War Two until it joined the allies in early 1945. It became a member of NATO in 1952 and is an associate member of the European Union. Turkey's quest for full membership of the Union is hampered by its poor human rights record, Greek objections and economic problems. Ankara applied for full EU membership in 1987 but was told to wait. \par \par \uc2  Development of multi-party democracy since the 1940s has been dogged by political squabbling, corruption and violence which led to military \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1960, 1971 and 1980. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly backed the U.S.-led coalition against Iraq during the Gulf crisis, when U.S. planes used Turkish bases. \par \par \uc2  The Cyprus issue has long dogged Turkish-Greek relations. Turkey invaded the island in 1974 to protect the Turkish-Cypriot minority after a Greek-backed coup, and has held the island's northern third ever since, keeping about 30,000 troops there. \par \par \uc2  Turkey upset Iraq and Syria in 1990 by diverting the Euphrates, which flows through all three, to fill a new dam. It has also been at odds with Iran, Iraq and Syria over their alleged support for the Kurdistan Workers Party rebel group. \par \par \uc2  Turkey captured the rebels' leader Abdullah Ocalan in 1999 and plans to try him for treason. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4i02m2a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\