\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Foreign Banks Leading Dlr Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 1999\par 14:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-buying demand led by foreign banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday afternoon, with the bank buying Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the lira's depreciation rate to its monthly 4.0% target against the dollar/euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said on the eve of Sunday's general and local elections, foreign institutional investors and some domestic banks are covering their short dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Market participants see political uncertainty after the elections with less chance of a market-friendly government. \par \par \uc2  Despite the uncertain political picture, the dollar demand was limited, traders said. They added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Friday. \par \par \uc2  For the longer term, market participants believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can successfully manage the lira exchange rate and they see no surprises. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the dollar closed at TRL379,400, up from Wednesday's close of TRL379,000. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 15-04-99 \par \par \uc2  1225GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4f02tx5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoa