\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Sparked By Banks' Dollar Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 165 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 1999\par 14:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Last-minute dollar-buying by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday afternoon, with the bank buying Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the lira's depreciation rate to a monthly 4% against the dollar/euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said foreign institutions are selling lira, lowering their exposures before the April 18 elections. \par \par \uc2  Local market participants don't see any imminent election-related political concern. \par \par \uc2  "However, a foreign fund manager can't justify holding a position for Turkey on the eve of elections," said an Istanbul-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Overall, traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can meet any dollar-buying demand until the election uncertainty settles. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the dollar closed at TRL375,800, up from Monday's close of TRL374,900. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay; +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 06-04-99 \par \par \uc2  1241GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4602mc5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
