\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Sparked By Banks' Dollar Buying \b0\par\par\uc2 238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 1999\par 14:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-buying by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  caused the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Monday afternoon, with the bank buying Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the lira's depreciation rate to a monthly 4% against the dollar and euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been expected but that they were unsure whether the dollar demand was isolated or marked the start of a general buying trend. \par \par \uc2  There was speculation earlier that some foreign institutions were switching from lira positions to dollars due to political uncertainty connected to the elections on April 18. \par \par \uc2  However, an Istanbul-based trader said that London markets, the main base for Turkey-related transactions, are closed Monday, therefore excluding the "foreign investor exodus" speculation. He said the domestic demand could be purely technical. \par \par \uc2  Local traders don't see any justification for a general exodus and see relative political stability in the post-election period. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the extent of the dollar demand would need to be watched. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling lira for dollars almost every day since Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  Monday, the dollar closed at TRL374,900, up from Friday's close of TRL373,100. \par \par \uc2 