\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Sparked By Banks' Dollar Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 14:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate to  0.13% against the dollar/euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was calm despite the recent political tensions as some rebel deputies attempt to postpone the April 18 elections, and the Islamist Virtue party challenges the judiciary and the secular establishment through a proposal to lift the ban on its former leader, Necmettin Erbakan. \par \par \uc2  Calm trading was also seen in the bond market as annual compound yields declined to 102%-103%, from Tuesday's 107%-108%. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, the dollar closed at TRL362,500, down from TRL363,500 at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-03-99 \par \par \uc2  1330GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3h024mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
