\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Lira Depreciation Slowing \b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 15:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate to a monthly 3.91% against the dollar/euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket from intraday lows. At Thursday's close, the depreciation rate was 3.92%. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the commercial banks were selling dollars to switch to lira instruments before interest rates decline any further. \par \par \uc2  Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its overnight and one-day lira lending upper limit to 77% from 79%. Traders said this would mean slower lira depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "Banks want to sell dollars when the depreciation rate is relatively high," said an Istanbul-based trader. "Also they want to catch the relatively high lira rates, before they decline any further." \par \par \uc2  Overall, traders see slower inflation. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is targeting an annual inflation rate of 45% for end-June. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the dollar closed at TRL358,300, up from TRL357,520 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 05-03-99 \par \par \uc2  1409GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3501r4o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pic