\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Prompted By Banks' Dollar Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1999\par 16:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate. The bank maintains a monthly 4.2% depreciation against the dollar/euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said foreign banks were also selling dollars to meet buying demand for lira-denominated bonds. \par \par \uc2  Market sentiment was positive Wednesday amid reports that the Turkish Treasury confirmed that it mandated Commerzbank AG and Deutsche Bank Tuesday to issue a Deutsche mark-denominated bond. No further details were given, but Turkey is looking to issue a four year Eurobond in Deutsche marks or euros for the equivalent of up to DEM500 million. \par \par \uc2  The presence of the International Monetary Fund team in Ankara for a routine review, also boosted sentiment. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the dollar closed at TRL329,160, up from Tuesday's close at TRL327,830. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 27-01-99 \par \par \uc2  1529GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1r00obd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
